/** \page cp-localisation Localisation

Localisation is an important concept in Marmalade. It allows users from different cultures to use Marmalade Engine.

Any string which is displayed in the user interface must be localised.

Marmalade uses the <a href="https://github.com/j-jorge/libintl-lite">libintl-lite</a> library for localisation.

<b>If you are a translator, skip to the \ref building-translations section.</b>

\section localising-strings Localising Strings

In the simplest manner, strings can be localised by wrapping the string in the `_( )` macro, defined in `application/i18n.h`:

```cpp
_("My string to be localised");
```

\subsection plural-forms Plural Forms

If a string is used a singular and plural forms, the `ngettext()` function should be used:

```cpp
ngettext("1 item added", "%d items added", n); // n is the number of items
```

The function takes three arguments: the singular string, the plural string (typically as a C-format string), and the
number. For more information, see the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/gettext/manual/html_node/Plural-forms.html">gettext guide on plural forms</a>.

\subsection contexts Contexts

Sometimes a string on its own might not be enough to provide a suitable translation. This is where contexts should be used.

A \ref translator-comments "Translator Comment" might be enough here, but if a string is used multiple times in different contexts, the `pgettext()`
function should be used:

```cpp
pgettext("File|", "Open");
pgettext("Printer|", "Open");
```

Here, the "Open" string could appear in the "File" menu or the "Printer menu". When using a menu as a context, make sure to include the trailing `|`.

For more information, see the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/gettext/manual/html_node/Contexts.html">gettext guide on contexts</a>.

\section translator-comments Translator Comments

Never hesitate to add additional comments for translators if a string could be unclear or ambiguous.

Comments for translators begin with "Translators: ":

```cpp
/* Translators: This is the verb, not the noun */
_("Shop")
```

Special comments, such as above `ngettext()` can also be used:

```cpp
/* xgettext: range: 1..6 */
ngettext("An item", "%d items", n);
```

\section pot-files POT Files

After adding a localisable string, run the `po/generate-pot.sh` script. This will scan the source files and create a POT
file.

A POT file is essentially a template file containing all the strings requiring translation. It is the starting point for
building translations.

\section testing-translations Testing Translations

It is important to ensure all strings displayed in the UI are localisable. This can be performed by providing a
pseudo-localisation.

First of all, make sure the POT file is up to date, see \ref pot-files. Then run the `tools/pseudo-localise.py` script.

Set the language inside Marmalade to `po@pseudo` and after a restart, any localisable string should render with accents
and additional characters.

\section building-translations Building Translations

First of all, make sure <a href="https://poedit.net/">Poedit</a> is installed.

Turn off "Automatically compile MO file when saving" in Poedit's Settings, since these are compiled by CMake during build.

\subsection new-language For a New Language

1. Open Poedit
2. Select "Create new" and select the `po/marmalade.pot` file.
3. Choose the language you want to add a translation for and click "OK".
4. Add translations for the strings.
5. Save the .po file to the `po` directory and reload CMake.

\subsection existing-language For an Existing Language

1. Open Poedit
2. Select "Browse files" and choose the relevant .po file in the `po` directory.
3. Choose Translation -> Update from POT file, and select the `po/marmalade.pot` file.
4. Any new strings will be displayed in Poedit.

*/
